{-
   The ASCII scanning tier - Implementation-Vector.md §9 item 8, Implementation-String.md part 5.

   The item the vector plan calls the highest-value one in it, and what it turned out to be is three
   library functions over machinery that was already built:

   - `alias CodeUnit`, target-selected the way `Size` is - a UTF-8 byte natively, a UTF-16 unit on JS;
   - `instance Chunked(String, CodeUnit)` natively, which is the sentence "a string is a container of
     code units" and is what puts a string on the vector path at all;
   - `findAscii`, `containsAscii` and `countAscii`, which are `indexOf` and `occurrences` over that
     instance - so the scan is the vector search the bulk operations already had, and nothing about
     it is string-specific.

   The three live in `Core/Text.yana` with the rest of the text tier that grew around them, and this
   file is the assertion that the *instance* under them is the vector path rather than a loop:
   `Text.test.yana` covers the surface, and what is here is one text per lane count, held to the same
   indices on both targets.

   None of this decodes anything, and it does not have to: an ASCII unit is self-synchronizing in
   both UTF-8 and UTF-16 - a value below 0x80 never appears inside the encoding of any other code
   point in either - so the same index means the same position in both builds for ASCII content.

   On JS these are `String.prototype.indexOf`, which Implementation-String.md part 5 rules is the
   right implementation rather than a compromise: one fixed unit has no room for the cross-engine
   disagreement the decoding tier has to worry about, and the engine's own C++ beats any `charCodeAt`
   loop this compiler would emit. Both targets are held to the same indices below, which is the whole
   point of the ASCII restriction.
-}

import Test

@test fn stringAsciiCases() -> {}:
    let line = "name,age,city"
    let text = "hello, world, and hello again"

    -- 44 is a comma. The first one is at index four in either encoding, because everything before it
    -- is ASCII.
    match findAscii(line, 44):
        Just(at) -> checkEqual(at, 4)
        Nothing -> check(False)

    checkEqual(countAscii(line, 44), 2)
    check(containsAscii(line, 44))

    -- 33 is `!`, which is not there. The absent case is a `Nothing` rather than a sentinel index.
    check(findAscii(line, 33) is Nothing)
    check(!containsAscii(line, 33))

    -- Long enough to leave a tail at every lane count: 29 units against sixteen or thirty-two.
    checkEqual(countAscii(text, 108), 5)

    match findAscii(text, 119):
        Just(w) -> checkEqual(w, 7)
        Nothing -> check(False)

    -- The first unit and the last, which are the two indices an off-by-one shows up in.
    match findAscii(text, 104):
        Just(first) -> checkEqual(first, 0)
        Nothing -> check(False)

    match findAscii(text, 110):
        Just(n) -> checkEqual(n, 15)
        Nothing -> check(False)
